<div class="ltx_dates">2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models have shown remarkable promise for image restoration by leveraging powerful priors. Prominent methods typically frame the restoration problem within a Bayesian inference framework, which iteratively combines a denoising step with a likelihood guidance step.
However, the interactions between these two components in the generation process remain underexplored.
In this paper, we analyze the underlying gradient dynamics of these components and identify significant instabilities. Specifically, we demonstrate conflicts between the prior and likelihood gradient directions, alongside temporal fluctuations in the likelihood gradient itself.
We show that these instabilities disrupt the generative process and compromise restoration performance.
To address these issues, we propose Stabilized Progressive Gradient Diffusion (SPGD), a novel gradient management technique. SPGD integrates two synergistic components: (1) a progressive likelihood warm-up strategy to mitigate gradient conflicts; and (2) adaptive directional momentum (ADM) smoothing to reduce fluctuations in the likelihood gradient. Extensive experiments across diverse restoration tasks demonstrate that SPGD significantly enhances generation stability, leading to state-of-the-art performance in quantitative metrics and visually superior results.
Code is available at <a href="https://github.com/74587887/SPGD" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">here</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S0.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="501.26" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 476 501.26" width="476"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,501.26) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(-0.28,0) translate(0,-0.28)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 0.28 0.28)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.4em;--ltx-fo-height:34.4em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="476.01" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 476.01)" width="476.01"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2507.06656v1/figs/cover.jpg" id="S0.F1.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 38.16 487.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="33.44"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S0.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">Input</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 164.87 486.12)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="27.67"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S0.F1.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">DPS</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 278.49 486.12)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.12em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="29.33"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S0.F1.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">Ours</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 387.85 486.04)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.21em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="58.27"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S0.F1.pic1.4" class="ltx_text">Reference</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustrative comparison of diffusion-based restoration results: DPS (without gradient management), Ours (with the proposed gradient management), and the reference. Our method demonstrates improved restoration quality, particularly in challenging restoration scenarios.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Image restoration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lehtinen et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, encompassing tasks such as inpainting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elharrouss et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, deblurring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, and super-resolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saharia et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, is a fundamental problem in computer vision and image processing. The goal is to recover a high-quality, clean image <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from a degraded measurement <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is often modeled by the following equation:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{x}_{0})+\mathbf{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐧</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{x}_{0})+\mathbf{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a known degradation operator (e.g., a blurring kernel, a downsampling matrix, or a masking operator), and <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},{\sigma_{y}^{2}}\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},{\sigma_{y}^{2}}\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> represents additive noise. Traditional approaches to solve this ill-posed inverse problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milanfar, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tai et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021</a>)</cite> often rely on handcrafted priors and regularization terms, which may not fully capture the complex statistics of natural images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021b</a>)</cite> have emerged as a transformative force in generative modeling, achieving state-of-the-art results in image synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poole et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saharia et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Their remarkable ability to model complex data distribution and generating realistic images, has naturally led to significant interest in adapting them for image restoration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lugmayr et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carrillo et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Among these methods, a dominant paradigm leverages the Bayesian framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dou and Song, 2024</a>)</cite>, guiding the reverse diffusion process using the conditional score <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>. This score elegantly decomposes via Bayes’ theorem into two key components:</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{y})}_{\text{Conditional Score}}=\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})}_{\text{Prior Score}}+\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})}_{\text{Likelihood Guidance}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Conditional Score</mtext></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Prior Score</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Likelihood Guidance</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{y})}_{\text{Conditional Score}}=\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})}_{\text{Prior Score}}+\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})}_{\text{Likelihood Guidance}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the prior score enforces conformity to the learned distribution of natural images, while the likelihood guidance ensures consistency with the observed measurement <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Existing methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> typically apply the gradients corresponding to the prior score and likelihood guidance iteratively, without explicitly accounting for their potentially detrimental interactions.
However, despite the success of these approaches, we argue that their performance is limited by inherent <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">conflicts and instabilities within the gradient dynamics</span> governing the reverse process. Our detailed analysis in <a href="#S3" title="3. A Gradient Perspective on Reverse Process ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which examines the reverse update from a gradient decomposition perspective, reveals two critical issues:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient Conflict:</span> The gradient enforcing data consistency (likelihood guidance) often conflicts with the gradient enforcing the natural image prior in direction, particularly in the early stages of generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient Fluctuation:</span> The likelihood guidance gradient exhibits significant temporal instability, fluctuating abruptly between consecutive timesteps.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Such issues can hinder the effective combination of prior knowledge and data fidelity, leading to suboptimal updates and ultimately compromising generation quality. Addressing these instabilities is essential for unlocking the full potential of diffusion models in demanding restoration tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stabilized Progressive Gradient Diffusion (SPGD)</span>, a novel gradient management technique designed to address these identified instabilities. SPGD integrates two synergistic components into the reverse diffusion step:</p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Progressive Likelihood Warm-Up:</span> To mitigate gradient conflict, we introduce multiple small, iterative updates guided only by the likelihood gradient before each main denoising step. This allows the estimate to progressively adapt to measurement constraints, reducing potential directional clashes with the prior gradient.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Directional Momentum (ADM) Smoothing:</span> To counteract likelihood gradient instability, we employ ADM smoothing. This technique dynamically adjusts momentum based on directional consistency, effectively dampening erratic fluctuations and promoting more stable update guidance from the likelihood term.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">These components work in concert to stabilize the reverse trajectory, leading to more effective and robust optimization, even with fewer computational resources.
Extensive experiments across diverse restoration tasks demonstrate that SPGD significantly enhances reconstruction quality, yielding substantial improvements in restoration metrics alongside visually superior results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our primary contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a novel gradient-centric perspective on the reverse process in diffusion models for image restoration, identifying the critical, yet previously under-addressed, issues of gradient conflict and fluctuation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose Stabilized Progressive Gradient Diffusion (SPGD), a novel gradient management technique incorporating a progressive warm-up phase and momentum-based smoothing to mitigate these issues.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments on various image restoration tasks across two different datasets, demonstrating that SPGD significantly improves reconstruction quality both quantitatively and qualitatively.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background</h2>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06656v1/Inpainting_Random_Column_2.svg" id="S2.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:115/79;" width="115" height="79"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06656v1/Gaussian_Deblur_Column_2.svg" id="S2.F2.sf1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:115/79;" width="115" height="79"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06656v1/Motion_Deblur_Column_2.svg" id="S2.F2.sf1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:115/79;" width="115" height="79"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06656v1/Inpainting_Random_Column_5.svg" id="S2.F2.sf1.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:115/79;" width="115" height="79"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S2.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gradient angles dynamics without smoothing.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06656v1/x1.svg" id="S2.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:115/79;" width="115" height="79"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06656v1/x2.svg" id="S2.F2.sf2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:115/79;" width="115" height="79"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06656v1/x3.svg" id="S2.F2.sf2.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:115/79;" width="115" height="79"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06656v1/x4.svg" id="S2.F2.sf2.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:115/79;" width="115" height="79"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S2.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gradient angles dynamics with ADM smoothing.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S2.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Angular relationships of gradients during the reverse process across different tasks, illustrating the gradient angles dynamics (a) without smoothing and (b) with our ADM smoothing. <span id="S2.F2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0073BD;">Blue lines</span>: angle between <math id="S2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S2.F2.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9521A;">Orange lines</span>: angle between <math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S2.F2.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009E73;">Green lines</span>: angle between <math id="S2.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>, also known as score-based models, operate by defining a stochastic process that gradually perturbs data samples into a noise distribution and subsequently learns to reverse this process to generate samples from the desired data distribution.
Consider a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-step forward process, where the noised sample <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> at time step <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be modeled from the previous state <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{t-1})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{t-1},\beta_{t}\mathbf{I})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{t-1})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{t-1},\beta_{t}\mathbf{I}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Gaussian distribution, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a pre-defined parameter increasing with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Through reparameterization, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be directly expressed in terms of the original data <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> via:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{0},(1-\bar{\alpha}_{t})\mathbf{I})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{0},(1-\bar{\alpha}_{t})\mathbf{I}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}_{t}=\prod_{i=0}^{t}\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}_{t}=\prod_{i=0}^{t}\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=1-\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=1-\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The training objective of diffusion models is typically defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{L}({\mathbf{\theta}})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim q(\mathbf{x}_{0}),\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}),t\sim\mathbb{U}(\{1,...,T\})}[\|\bm{\epsilon}-\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)\|^{2}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>𝕌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{L}({\mathbf{\theta}})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim q(\mathbf{x}_{0}),\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}),t\sim\mathbb{U}(\{1,...,T\})}[\|\bm{\epsilon}-\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)\|^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from training data and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim q(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim q(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Once the network <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> is trained, a clean sample can be derived by evaluating the generative reverse process step by step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth noting that one can adopt DDIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021a</a>)</cite> to perform the reverse sampling, as it preserves the same marginal distributions <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> as DDPM. The DDIM sampling procedure can be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}=\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t})+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}\mathbf{\bm{\epsilon}}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}=\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t})+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}\mathbf{\bm{\epsilon}}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)+\sigma_{t}\bm{\epsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t})=(\mathbf{x}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t))/{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t})=(\mathbf{x}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t))/{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted clean image given <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> governs the variance of the reverse sampling process. Specifically, setting <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=0</annotation></semantics></math> yields a deterministic DDIM sampling, while <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\cdot\beta_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\cdot\beta_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the original DDPM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Diffusion-Based Solvers for Image Restoration</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The strong generative power of diffusion models makes them excellent candidates for solving image restoration and inverse problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
To implement this, the reverse process is guided by the conditional score <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in <a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, this involves the unconditional score <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and the likelihood term <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The unconditional score <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is directly related to <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{-\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{-\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>. However, the likelihood term is intractable because <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown, since there only exists an explicit connection between <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, not <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. To deal with this, a family of methods avoids explicit likelihood computation through interleaving optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> or projecting the estimate onto a measurement-consistent subspace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kawar et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cardoso et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The another category of methods direct compute the likelihood under some mild assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rout et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rout et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Of these,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al. (2023)</a></cite> propose Diffusion Posterior Sampling (DPS) to approximate the likelihood using a Laplacian approximation:
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})\simeq p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})\simeq p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted clean image of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> via Tweedie’s formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stein, 1981</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Efron, 2011</a>)</cite>.
Then the likelihood gradient can be calculated by:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})\simeq-{\zeta}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}_{t})\simeq-{\zeta}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> is a tunable step size that controls the strength of the guidance.
The complete DPS algorithm with DDIM deterministic update is shown below:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\big(\frac{\mathbf{x}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}\big)+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\big(\frac{\mathbf{x}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}\big)+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{\prime}-{\zeta}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{\prime}-{\zeta}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By iteratively updating via likelihood matching combined with denoising, one can effectively recover high-quality estimates of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from degraded observations <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, as we will show, the direct application of these updates can be unstable.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>A Gradient Perspective on Reverse Process</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Reverse Update Decomposition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the empirical success of Bayesian guidance frameworks, the underlying behavior and interaction between the prior score and likelihood guidance warrant closer examination. In this section, we adopt a gradient-centric perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinh et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to analyze the reverse update process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Consider the DPS likelihood guidance combined with the deterministic DDIM update (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=0</annotation></semantics></math>), we rearrange the update from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to explicitly highlight the contributing gradient terms. The insightful decomposition (see <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Derivation for Reverse Update Decomposition ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for derivation) is:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{t-1}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{t-1}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{x}_{t}}_{\text{fixed scaling}}-\underbrace{\left(\frac{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sqrt{{\alpha}_{t}}}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\right)\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)}_{\text{denoising gradient}~(\mathbf{g}_{d})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>fixed scaling</mtext></munder><mo>−</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mtext>denoising gradient</mtext><mo lspace="0.270em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{x}_{t}}_{\text{fixed scaling}}-\underbrace{\left(\frac{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sqrt{{\alpha}_{t}}}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\right)\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)}_{\text{denoising gradient}~(\mathbf{g}_{d})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-{\zeta}\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{likelihood gradient}~(\mathbf{g}_{l})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mtext>likelihood gradient</mtext><mo lspace="0.270em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-{\zeta}\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{likelihood gradient}~(\mathbf{g}_{l})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Here, we explicitly define:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>: The <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">denoising gradient</span>, derived from the learned prior <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, guiding the sample towards the natural image manifold.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>: The <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">likelihood gradient</span>, enforcing consistency with the measurement <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> via the reconstruction error.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The fixed scaling term primarily adjusts the magnitude of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The core dynamics influencing the image content arise from the interplay between <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. While previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> implicitly combine these effects, analyzing their individual dynamics reveals potential sources of performance degradation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Gradient Conflicts and Fluctuations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically examine the angular relationships between <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as the temporal consistency of each gradient. The results are shown in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2. Background ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (see <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E More Visual Results ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for more results). Our analysis reveals two critical issues:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(1) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient Conflict:</span> The angle between <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0073BD;">blue lines</span> in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2. Background ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) frequently diverges from 90 degrees in the early stage, implying that the prior direction conflicts with the data constraint direction. While these conflicts diminish as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> decreases <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            two randomly chosen vectors tend to be orthogonal in high dimensions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledoux, 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talagrand, 1995</a>)</cite></span></span></span>, their presence in the early stages can disrupt the image structure formation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Everaert et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, a crucial phase for overall reconstruction quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2) <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Likelihood Gradient Fluctuation:</span> The temporal consistency between <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9521A;">orange lines</span> in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2. Background ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) often exhibits pronounced instability in the intermediate stages.
We speculate that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is derived from <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be highly sensitive to small changes in the estimate <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
These erratic fluctuations hinder smooth convergence towards data consistency and can potentially amplify errors over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the denoising gradient <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> derived from the well-trained <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrates remarkable temporal stability (visualized by the <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009E73;">green lines</span> in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2. Background ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), providing a consistent guidance from the learned prior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These observed conflicts and fluctuations in the gradient dynamics are not merely theoretical concerns; they manifest as tangible artifacts in reconstructed images, slower convergence rates, and quantifiable reductions in evaluation metrics (see <a href="#S5" title="5. Experiments ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). This motivates the development of a method that explicitly manages these gradient dynamics for more stable and effective restoration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Proposed Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by our analysis in <a href="#S3" title="3. A Gradient Perspective on Reverse Process ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we propose <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stabilized Progressive Gradient Diffusion (SPGD)</span>, a novel gradient management technique to enhance the stability and effectiveness of the reverse diffusion process. SPGD introduces two synergistic components: a progressive likelihood warm-up strategy and a momentum-based likelihood gradient smoothing technique.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Progressive Warm-Up Phase</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate the conflicts between the denoising gradient <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and the likelihood gradient <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, SPGD introduces a <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">progressive warm-up</span> phase for likelihood update before each primary denoising step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, within a single conceptual reverse step from time <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>, starting from the current estimate <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}=\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}=\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we perform <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> iterative refinement steps focusing solely on the likelihood gradient using a reduced step size:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j+1)}=\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}-\frac{\zeta}{N}\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}),\quad\text{for }j=0,1,\ldots,N-1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>ζ</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>for </mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j+1)}=\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}-\frac{\zeta}{N}\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}),\quad\text{for }j=0,1,\ldots,N-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math> is the ADM-smoothed likelihood gradient (detailed in <a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.2. Momentum-Based Likelihood Gradient Smoothing ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta/N</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a small learning rate for each warm-up step. This multi-step process allows the estimate <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to gradually adapt towards satisfying the measurement constraint <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">before</span> the potentially conflicting denoising gradient <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied. Moreover, the small step size helps prevent drastic deviations from the learned data manifold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alkhouri et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, fostering a more controlled adjustment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After these <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> warm-up iterations yield <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the standard denoising update is applied to obtain the initial estimate for the next timestep, using <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}</annotation></semantics></math> as input:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{(0)}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}-\left(\frac{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sqrt{{\alpha}_{t}}}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\right)\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)},t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{(0)}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}-\left(\frac{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sqrt{{\alpha}_{t}}}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\right)\bm{\epsilon}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)},t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is a standard denoising update with diffusion model, i.e. DDIM in this paper.
Given the observed stability of the denoising gradient <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, this multi-step warm-up mitigates the instability risk arising from potential conflicts, providing a more refined input <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}</annotation></semantics></math> for the reliable denoising operation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Stabilized Progressive Gradient Diffusion</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Measurements <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, degrade operator <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, network <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, parameters <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             Initialize <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T,\dots,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T,\dots,1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0,\dots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0,\dots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}))\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}))\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> j=0 <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
                <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})=\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})=\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>


</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
                <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}=(\text{sim}\big(\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)}),\,\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})\big)+1)/2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}=(\text{sim}\big(\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)}),\,\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})\big)+1)/2,</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
                <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})=\alpha_{j}\beta\,\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)})+(1-\alpha_{j}\beta)\,\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})=\alpha_{j}\beta\,\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)})+(1-\alpha_{j}\beta)\,\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#666666;">// adaptive momentum-based gradient smoothing</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j+1)}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}-\frac{\zeta}{N}\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>ζ</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j+1)}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}-\frac{\zeta}{N}\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#666666;">// warm-up likelihood update</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)})=\left({\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}/{\sqrt{{\alpha}_{t}}}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\right)\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)})=\left({\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}/{\sqrt{{\alpha}_{t}}}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\right)\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)},t)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{(0)}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}-\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{(0)}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}-\mathbf{g}_{d}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)})</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#666666;">// DDIM denoising update</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Momentum-Based Likelihood Gradient Smoothing</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To counteract the temporal fluctuation observed in the likelihood gradient <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, SPGD incorporates an <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Directional Momentum (ADM)</span> smoothing technique. This produces the stabilized likelihood gradient <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> by incorporating historical information, which is then used in the warm-up phase in <a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.1. Progressive Warm-Up Phase ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Standard momentum methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Polyak, 1964</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutskever et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> use an exponentially weighted moving average to smooth gradients. However, constant momentum can slow adaptation when the optimal direction genuinely changes. Our proposed ADM enhances this by dynamically adjusting the momentum strength based on the directional consistency between the current gradient and the accumulated momentum.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}))\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}))\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the raw likelihood gradient at warm-up step <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. The ADM-smoothed gradient <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math> is computed recursively:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})=\alpha_{j}\beta\,\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)})+(1-\alpha_{j}\beta)\,\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})=\alpha_{j}\beta\,\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)})+(1-\alpha_{j}\beta)\,\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with initialization <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})=\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})=\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the pre-defined base momentum coefficient. The key component is the adaptive weight <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}=\frac{\text{sim}\big(\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)}),\,\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})\big)+1}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}=\frac{\text{sim}\big(\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)}),\,\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})\big)+1}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v})=\frac{\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}}{\|\mathbf{u}\|\|\mathbf{v}\|}\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v})=\frac{\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}}{\|\mathbf{u}\|\|\mathbf{v}\|}\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, denotes the cosine similarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical intuition:</span>
When the current gradient <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math> aligns well (<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(\cdot)\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(\cdot)\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>) with the previous momentum <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and ADM behaves like standard momentum, smoothing the update by trusting the historical trend.
Conversely, when the directions differ significantly (<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(\cdot)\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(\cdot)\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases towards 0, effectively down-weighting the potentially outdated historical momentum and prioritizing the current gradient information.
By adaptively modulating the momentum, ADM effectively dampens erratic gradient fluctuations while remaining responsive to significant directional shifts, yielding a more stable and reliable gradient <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbf{g}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for guiding the optimization towards data consistency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The complete SPGD procedure is detailed in <a href="#alg1" title="In 4.1. Progressive Warm-Up Phase ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. In our experiments, we extend the warm-up strategy throughout the entire reverse process, and set the total number of outer diffusion steps <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=100</annotation></semantics></math> and warm-up steps <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5</annotation></semantics></math>, aligning with common Number of Function Evaluations (NFEs) in existing literature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Theoretical Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we provide theoretical support for our methodology. Our analysis highlights how the SPGD warm-up optimizes for data fidelity, distinguishing it from prior posterior sampling techniques.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06656v1/manifold_cropped.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/256;" width="476" height="256" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">High-level illustration of our proposed SPGD. (a) the standard reverse process (Eq. <a href="#S2.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 2.2. Diffusion-Based Solvers for Image Restoration ‣ 2. Background ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), and (b) our proposed SPGD (<a href="#alg1" title="In 4.1. Progressive Warm-Up Phase ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), showing the warm-up phase with smoothed gradient to enhance restoration stability.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_theorem">Proposition 4.1.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})=\frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})=\frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{y}-\mathcal{A}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the likelihood objective at diffusion timestep <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume the likelihood gradient <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}L_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}L_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuous with respect to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the SPGD warm-up phase without momentum (<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math>) using <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> steps with step size <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\zeta/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\zeta/N</annotation></semantics></math>. If the step size satisfies <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&lt;1/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&lt;1/L</annotation></semantics></math>, then the state <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained after <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> warm-up steps guarantees a decrease in the likelihood objective:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)})\leq L_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})-\eta\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\left(1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\right)\|\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})\|_{2}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)})\leq L_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)})-\eta\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\left(1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\right)\|\mathbf{g}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)})\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the initial state at time <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> before the warm-up.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Please refer to <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proofs ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.3. Theoretical Analysis ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> formally supports that the SPGD warm-up iterations progressively decrease the likelihood objective <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby enhancing the data consistency of the state <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> before denoising. In other words, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a better estimate <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with smaller likelihood objective value compared to using the initial state <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As conceptually illustrated in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.3. Theoretical Analysis ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ Enhancing Diffusion Model Stability for Image Restorationvia Gradient Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>,
SPGD contrasts with standard posterior sampling methods like DPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In DPS, the likelihood gradient <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed based on the initial state <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalent to our <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>), but the correction is applied only <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">after</span> the denoising step produces <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This timing mismatch, where a gradient relevant to <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> corrects the transformed state <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, can potentially lead to instability and cause trajectories to deviate from the data manifold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
On the contrary, SPGD performs iterative refinement directly on <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> before invoking the denoiser. By providing the denoising step with an input <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(N)}</annotation></semantics></math> that is already better aligned with the measurements <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, SPGD potentially facilitates a more stable update process and a more accurate subsequent generation.</p>
</div>
