<td id="S4.T1.7.1.22.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.22.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FA;">25.71</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.22.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.22.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FA;">0.705</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.22.13" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.22.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FA;">0.203</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets &amp; Metrics.</span> We evaluate our proposed SPGD method and baseline approaches on 1,000 images from the FFHQ 256<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>256 validation set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and 500 images from the ImageNet 256<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>256 validation set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russakovsky et al., 2015</a>)</cite>. The pre-trained diffusion models for FFHQ and ImageNet are obtained from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>)</cite>, respectively, following established conventions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Performance is assessed with quantitative metrics: Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Structural Similarity Index Measure (SSIM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2004</a>)</cite> for distortion evaluation, and Learned Perceptual Image Patch Similarity (LPIPS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2018</a>)</cite> for perceptual quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> We compare our method against several state-of-the-art diffusion-based image restoration techniques, including PnP-ADMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chan et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, Score-SDE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, MCG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, DDRM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kawar et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, DPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, DiffPIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, DPPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, and RED-Diff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mardani et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. All experiments are conducted using the same pre-trained models and a fixed random seed to ensure fair comparisons.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tasks.</span> We consider four standard linear image restoration tasks:
(1) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inpainting:</span> Randomly mask 80% of the pixels across all RGB channels.
(2) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gaussian Blur:</span> Apply a Gaussian blur kernel of size <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="61\times 61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>61</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>61</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">61\times 61</annotation></semantics></math> with a standard deviation of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=3.0</annotation></semantics></math>.
(3) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion Blur:</span> Use a motion blur kernel of size <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="61\times 61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>61</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>61</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">61\times 61</annotation></semantics></math> and intensity 0.5, following the procedure of Chung et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
(4) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Super-Resolution (SR) <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>4:</span> Perform <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> bicubic downsampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In all scenarios, Gaussian noise with a variance of <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> is added to the degraded measurements.
For efficiency, all our ablation studies were conducted using 100 FFHQ test images.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation study on the effect of components.</span></figcaption>
